<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rijali</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">17th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Imam Rijali</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rijali was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> from Hitu on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambon-island">Ambon Island</a> who wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hikayat-tanah-hitu">Hikayat Tanah Hitu</a>, a Malay chronicle of Hitu, in the mid-17th century. Opposed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rule, he spent years in exile, including at Makassar. His work traces the origins of the Hitu rulers to settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Moluccas</a> and is a key source for the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambon">Ambon</a> in the period of Dutch expansion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rijali.jpg" alt="Rijali" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bakkouz at Arabic Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hadith_Books.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
